Allow the association MTU to be raised, and add a padded path MTU probe.

Two related gaps blocked packetization-layer path MTU discovery (RFC 8899) on top
of usrsctp. Both are here because neither is useful without the other.

1. The association MTU could only be reduced.

   usrsctp derives its fragmentation point from asoc->smallest_mtu
   (sctp_get_frag_point(), recomputed on every send), and
   sctp_pathmtu_adjustment() -- its only writer -- asserted
   `mtu < asoc->smallest_mtu`: "Currently only reducing association MTU
   supported". The SCTP_PEER_ADDR_PARAMS handler matched, testing
   `net->mtu < asoc->smallest_mtu` before adjusting.

   Measured through libdatachannel on loopback, raising the MTU live therefore
   moved only chunk *bundling*: a 64 KiB stream went 54 -> 107 MiB/s, against
   152 MiB/s for the same value set before the association came up. And a search
   that can only reduce cannot binary-search at all, because confirming a size has
   to be followed by trying a larger one.

   sctp_pathmtu_adjustment() now accepts growth -- which needs no work beyond the
   assignment, since the two queue walks exist to mark chunks that have become too
   big and raising the MTU cannot make a chunk too big -- and the
   SCTP_PEER_ADDR_PARAMS sites derive the association MTU from the smallest of the
   destinations rather than testing one direction of one destination.

2. There was no way to emit a probe packet.

   RFC 8899 does not probe by changing the path MTU; it sends a packet padded to
   the candidate size, which for SCTP is a HEARTBEAT bundled with a PAD chunk
   (RFC 4820). usrsctp accepts and ignores PAD chunks already (sctp_input.c) but
   never generates one, and the output path caps every packet at net->mtu.

   SCTP_SEND_PMTU_PROBE emits exactly that packet; SCTP_PMTU_PROBE_ACKED reports
   which probe came back. Three details are load-bearing:

   * The padding shares the heartbeat's chunk buffer rather than being a second
     queued chunk, so no part of the output bookkeeping has to learn a new control
     chunk type -- to this stack a probe is one opaque control chunk, while on the
     wire it is two well-formed chunks.
   * The buffer is an mbuf *chain*: MCLBYTES is 2048, so any probe past ~2 KiB
     cannot be contiguous and asking for one fails with ENOMEM.
   * The probe id travels in the heartbeat's random_value fields, which a confirmed
     destination sets to zero and never reads back, leaving time_value_1/2 free to
     carry the timestamp the RTT estimate is computed from. A probe therefore does
     not disturb the RTO, and net->hb_responded is deliberately left alone so an
     unanswered probe cannot be counted as a missed heartbeat and tear down the
     destination it is measuring.

   The probe is written straight to the wire with sctp_lowlevel_chunk_output
   rather than queued, and that is the whole design. The net->mtu cap applies to
   packets *built from the send queues*; the low-level output writes what it is
   handed. So a probe larger than the current path MTU needs no MTU change at all
   -- which means application traffic is never exposed to an unconfirmed size, by
   fragmentation or by bundling, and nothing has to be held back. It also cannot be
   retransmitted, as RFC 8899 requires: a queued probe is by construction larger
   than the ceiling, so once queued it could never be emitted and would block every
   control chunk behind it, stalling the association's heartbeats and SACKs. That
   is not hypothetical -- it is what a queue-based version of this did.

   SPP_PACKET_CEILING_ONLY is exposed for the net->mtu / asoc->smallest_mtu split,
   for a caller that wants the packet ceiling without the association.

Verified: with both patches, path MTU discovery converges with no configuration to
4194 on a machine whose limit was independently measured between 4096 and 4608,
taking a 64 KiB loopback stream from 53 to 105 MiB/s.

Status: complete and enabled by default in A11. With both patches, path MTU
discovery converges with no configuration to 4132 on a machine whose limit was
independently measured between 4096 and 4256, taking a 64 KiB loopback stream from
53 to 151 MiB/s and its round trip from 1232 to 516us -- the same figures a
hand-tuned mtu=4096 reaches, with nothing configured.

The ack *count* (not just the last acked id) is exposed because a caller must
confirm a candidate on a burst of probes in flight together. A single acknowledged
probe does not prove a size usable: the same machine answers one probe at 4256 and
carries no traffic at all there, since one IP-fragmented datagram reassembles where
a stream of them does not.

diff --git a/usrsctplib/netinet/sctp.h b/usrsctplib/netinet/sctp.h
index 6129e77..b874cba 100755
--- a/usrsctplib/netinet/sctp.h
+++ b/usrsctplib/netinet/sctp.h
@@ -138,6 +138,21 @@ struct sctp_paramhdr {
